                        "reasoning": "The setup path allocates an operation and returns success after psa_aead_*_setup() succeeds, relying on out_vec[0].base to communicate the newly allocated handle to the caller. This file validates only that out_vec[0] is non-NULL and large enough before allocation; it does not include a rollback path for cases where the secure-service return or final write-back to the client fails after the allocation succeeds. In firmware PSA IPC designs, output vectors often cross a privilege boundary, and a client-provided or invalidated output buffer can cause the handle not to be delivered even though the secure partition has consumed an operation slot. If such a late copy/write-back failure is possible in the surrounding framework, repeated setup calls can orphan operation contexts and exhaust the crypto operation pool. This candidate depends on the IPC marshalling semantics outside this file, but the source shows the lifetime coupling between allocation and caller-visible handle delivery without local compensation.",
                        "mitigation": "Where possible, allocate the operation only after the IPC layer has pinned/validated the destination for the handle, or provide a framework-level cleanup hook that releases newly allocated operations if the final response copy to the client fails. Keep the allocated handle in secure-owned state until successful write-back is guaranteed, or design the handle return path so allocation and handle publication are atomic with respect to IPC failure.",
