                        "reasoning": "The file validates that asymmetric signing and key-derivation modules have at least one corresponding algorithm enabled, but this check is not paired with any requirement that private key types needed to use those algorithms are enabled. The only key-type prerequisite check earlier in the file accepts public asymmetric key types only (`PSA_WANT_KEY_TYPE_ECC_PUBLIC_KEY`, `PSA_WANT_KEY_TYPE_RSA_PUBLIC_KEY`, `PSA_WANT_KEY_TYPE_DH_PUBLIC_KEY`) and does not mention private RSA/ECC/DH key types. In a cross-configuration where signing or ECDH/FFDH is compiled while private key-type support is omitted, the resulting firmware may have mismatched PSA crypto capability gating. Depending on surrounding dispatch code, that can become a privilege-boundary/configuration issue: clients may be exposed to operations whose key-type validation or backend support was not actually compiled, potentially leading to inconsistent authorization/denial behavior or latent unsafe error paths. The reviewed source does not prove runtime reachability, but the configuration validation is incomplete for private-key operations.",
